no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place (position4):\n\nFirst number:6, second:7.\n\n6+7=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place (position5):\n\nFirst number:3, second:2.\n\n3+2=5 +1 (carryover)=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place (position6):\n\nFirst number:4, second:3.\n\n4+3=7. Write7, no carryover.\n\n7. Millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number:3, second:8.\n\n3+8=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number:7, second:3.\n\n7+3=10 +1 (carryover)=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions place (position9):\n\nFirst number:6, second:5.\n\n6+5=11 +1 (carryover)=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\n10. Billions place (position10):\n\nNo digits here except the carryover1. So write1.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left, the digits are:\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition5:6\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition7:1\n\nPosition8:1\n\nPosition9:2\n\nPosition10:1\n\nSo the number is 1 2 1 1 7 6 3 9 8 4 ?\n\nWait, wait, no. Wait, the positions are from right to left. So the digits are:\n\nStarting from the rightmost (units):\n\nPosition1 (units):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7\n\nPosition7 (millions):1\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2\n\nPosition10 (billions):1\n\nTherefore, when written from left to right (billions to units):\n\n1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (units).\n\nWait, that would be 1,211,763,984?\n\nWait, let me check again:\n\nThe digits from highest to lowest:\n\nPosition10:1 (billions place)\n\nPosition9:2 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:1 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:1 (millions)\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:8 (tens)\n\nPosition1:4 (units)\n\nSo combining them:\n\n1,2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (units). So written as 1,211,763,984.\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Let's add the two numbers using another approach.\n\n673,436,698\n\n+538,327,286\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n673,436,698 is 673 million plus 436,698.\n\n538,327,286 is 538 million plus 327,286.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 673 +538 = 1,211 million.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 436,698 + 327,286.\n\n436,698 + 327,286:\n\nAdding those:\n\n436,698\n\n+327,286\n\nUnits:8+6=14 →4, carryover1\n\nTens:9+8+1=18 →8, carryover1\n\nHundreds:6+2+1=9 →9\n\nThousands:6+7=13 →3, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+2=5 +1=6 →6\n\nHundred thousands:4+3=7 →7\n\nSo that gives 763,984.\n\nTherefore total is 1,211,000,000 +763,984 = 1,211,763,984.\n\nOkay, so that matches the previous result. So the answer is 1,211,763,984.\n\nWait, but let me confirm once more with actual digit addition:\n\n673,436,698\n\n+538,327,286\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:8+6=14 →4, carryover1\n\nTens:9+8=17 +1=18 →8, carryover1\n\nHundreds:6+2=8 +1=9 →9, no carryover.\n\nThousands:6+7=13 →3, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+2=5 +1=6 →6\n\nHundred thousands:4+3=7 →7\n\nMillions:3+8=11 →1, carryover1\n\nTen millions:7+3=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:6+5=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1\n\nBillions:0+0 +1=1 →1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:8\n\nUnits:4\n\nWhich is 1,211,763,984. That seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 1,211,763,984.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the sum of 805017775 and 012772194?  .\n# Reasoning:  .\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.  .\n0. The ones place: 3 + 2 = 22 → **6** (carryover **2**).  .\n7. The t